starts `ard serve --loops scanners,stale,reaper` ([#187](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/187)), so the triage, worker and reviewer loops never run there, even though `.ard/config.yaml` on `main` has agent roles. `--loops` takes a comma-separated list of loop names (scanners, stale, triage, workers, reviewers, reaper, budget), defaults to every loop, and rejects an unknown name with exit 2. `ard serve` and `ard config check` refuse to run on git older than 2.47.0, because the committer's fetches fail on older versions.\n \n A worker job is several executor jobs (`<id>`, `<id>t1`, `<id>f1`, ...) under one state job and one slot; the Reaper watches whichever step is stored on the job, so its heartbeat and deadline rules apply to tests too.\n \n-**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected.\n+**One pool of job slots, drained from the end.** At most `max_pods` jobs (default 3) run at once, shared by every loop. When a slot frees, the controller fills it in this order: reviewers, then workers, then triage, then scanners, oldest first within each. Finishing work in flight beats starting new work, so a busy day grows the scanner backlog, not the pile of half-done PRs. The merger and the Reaper are controller code and take no slot. Slots are granted when a loop polls and nothing is queued: a loop that found work waiting within the last two of its intervals has that demand reserved ahead of lower classes, so a freed slot can sit idle until that loop's next poll. A scanner run that gets no slot is skipped, not queued. The pool also starts no agent job for an exhausted account or model family until its reset: a job that stops at a subscription usage limit records the limit (in the controller database, so a restart keeps it), and the pool answers `limited` to any candidate whose role runs on that account or family (a whole-account session or weekly limit blocks every role; an Opus or Sonnet limit blocks only roles on that family, and a role with no model, which runs the CLI default, is blocked by any limit). A limited candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand, so lower classes are not held back. The block ends at the reset time the CLI reported; when it reported none the first pause is 30 minutes and each further stop with no reset doubles it up to 4 hours, starting over once a job succeeds; a reset time further away than 8 days is clamped. Running jobs are never killed for a limit. Deterministic scanners and the merger use no model and are unaffected. A daily dollar cap works the same way at the same gate: the pool answers `budget` to a candidate whose role cap or the repo cap is reached, and that candidate takes no slot and reserves no demand. A job that already holds a slot keeps it and finishes.\n \n |  | Local executor | Kubernetes executor |\n | --- | --- | --- |\n@@ -391,7 +391,7 @@ max_pods: 3                  # job slots shared by every loop\n executor: local              # local | k8s\n agent: claude                # default CLI for agent roles\n require_approval: true       # human adds ard:approved before a worker starts\n-budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\n+budget_usd_per_day: 50       # whole repo, UTC day, blocks at spent >= cap; a role may set its own; provisional, recalibrated between Gate 1 and Gate 2\n max_budget_usd: 5            # per job; provisional, same recalibration\n global_max_open: 30          # open scanner issues across all scanners\n paused: false\n@@ -419,7 +419,7 @@ overrides:                   # only what differs from convention\n \n **Models are tiered (decided, #8).** Opus 5.5 for the lead, architect and security review; Sonnet 5.5 for workers, triage engineer and most reviewers; Haiku 5.5 for lint and docs work. The `budget_usd_per_day: 50` and per-job `max_budget_usd` values are provisional and are recalibrated from measured cost per job and per merged PR between Gate 1 and Gate 2.\n \n-Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts and 3 review rounds. Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day`, `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n+Anything unset takes its default: intake in `label` mode with `ard:pending`, scanners every 4 h with `max_open` 5 and `group_size` 10, triage every 15 min, a 30-minute job timeout, `make test`, 2 test-fix attempts and 3 review rounds. Durations accept `m`, `h` and `d`. A sidecar `repo` must be `owner/name` and its `ref` a valid git ref name (no `:`, leading `-`, `..` or spaces; an abbreviated SHA is rejected, use all 40 characters); `ard config check` reports the file and key. A worker's `timeout` and `max_budget_usd` cover the whole job (every step), and `tests: \"\"` disables its test step. Per-role keys are `every`, `max_open`, `group_size`, `autoclose`, `timeout`, `agent`, `model`, `max_budget_usd`, `budget_usd_per_day` (that role's own daily cap in USD; unset or 0 means only the repo cap applies), `sidecar`, `after`, `plan_section`, `tests`, `reviewers` and `when` (`paths`, or `triage` roles that ran; at least one is required). `ard config check` warns when every reviewer has a `when:` rule, because a PR matching none gets no review and stays a draft. `plan_section` is for triage roles: the `## <heading>` of `plan.md` the role owns (one line, no leading `#`, unique across triage roles). The starter roles default to Requirements (product-manager), Design (architect), UX (designer), Docs (documentation-planner) and Implementation (engineer), and a value in `config.yaml` overrides that; a triage role with none may write only its own role file. There are no per-role config files: `ard init` writes a role's starting overrides into `config.yaml` for the roles it ships. Once a second repo adopts ard, role directories can move to a shared roles repo pinned by ref, like a sidecar; a repo's own directory of the same name wins. `after` names triage roles, with no self-reference and no cycles.\n \n **`ard init` proposes the starter `.ard/` as a PR (decided, #27).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer` and `reviewers/eng-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, docs, error-codes, lead, engineer, worker and eng-review overrides), compares them with `main`, commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR. It writes nothing to the local working tree or to `main`, and `scan` is committed `100755`. Files that already exist on `main` with different contents are a conflict naming the paths; `--force` proposes overwriting them, and init never deletes anything. It refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits: merge or close the PR, delete the branch and rerun. If `main` already has every starter file it prints `nothing to propose`. It syncs the label catalogue first (like `ard labels sync`), so a failure leaves nothing pushed. With `--dry-run` the commit, push and PR are logged to the ledger instead of acting. A missing repo, credentials or `main` branch exits 1 with nothing written, and `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` auth is refused outside `--dry-run`. The PR body carries the one step the App cannot do: copy `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` and set the repository variable `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` (optionally `ARD_BOT_CHECK_SINCE`). It also says to merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit**, never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history. **Rebase and merge** is also safe: the scratch-repo run (#85) showed GitHub records the merging human as committer of a rebase-merged commit (the bot stays the author), and the bot-commit check keys on committer, so it does not flag it. The body still names only the two merges above.\n \n@@ -441,7 +441,7 @@ Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit\n - **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, which markdown renders as `#12`; \"fixed a typo in #12's test\" is left alone. Only ard's own `Closes #<issue>` on the worker PR closes an issue.\n - **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\n - **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n-- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. At the cap the controller stops claiming and posts once on the status issue.\n+- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. `--loops` accepts `budget` as a name, but the gate is part of the pool and is always on. Posting the cap on the status issue is not part of this change.\n - **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n - **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n - **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths.\ndiff --git a/internal/budget/budget.go b/internal/budget/budget.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..05f3613\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/budget/budget.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,119 @@\n+// Package budget enforces the daily dollar caps at the pool's claim gate.\n+//\n+// The day is the UTC day. A candidate is refused when spent >= cap, for its\n+// role's own cap (budget_usd_per_day on the role) or the repo cap. Steps that\n+// reported no cost count as $0 and their number is shown. Reviewers get no\n+// exemption; deterministic scanners cost nothing and are exempt. A job that\n+// already holds a slot is never touched: the gate only sees new candidates.\n+// A state read failure denies (fails closed).\n+package budget\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"context\"\n+\t\"fmt\"\n+\t\"log/slog\"\n+\t\"sync\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/pool\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state\"\n+)\n+\n+// SpendReader reads today's spend; *state.Store satisfies it.\n+type SpendReader interface {\n+\tSpendSince(ctx context.Context, dayStart time.Time) (map[state.RoleKey]state.Spend, state.Spend, error)\n+}\n+\n+// Gate is the pool's Options.Budget hook.\n+type Gate struct {\n+\tstore SpendReader\n+\tcfg   func() *config.Resolved\n+\tnow   func() time.Time\n+\tlog   *slog.Logger\n+\n+\tmu     sync.Mutex\n+\tday    time.Time       // UTC day start the logged map belongs to\n+\tlogged map[string]bool // caps already logged on day\n+}\n+\n+// New builds a Gate. cfg returns the latest config (nil: nothing is capped);\n+// now nil means time.Now; log nil means slog.Default.\n+func New(store SpendReader, cfg func() *config.Resolved, now func() time.Time, log *slog.Logger) *Gate {\n+\tif now == nil {\n+\t\tnow = time.Now\n+\t}\n+\tif log == nil {\n+\t\tlog = slog.Default()\n+\t}\n+\treturn &Gate{store: store, cfg: cfg, now: now, log: log, logged: map[string]bool{}}\n+}\n+\n+func kindOf(c pool.Class) (config.Kind, string, bool) {\n+\tswitch c {\n+\tcase pool.ClassScanner:\n+\t\treturn config.KindScanner, c.String(), true\n+\tcase pool.ClassTriage:\n+\t\treturn config.KindTriage, c.String(), true\n+\tcase pool.ClassWorker:\n+\t\treturn config.KindWorker, c.String(), true\n+\tcase pool.ClassReview:\n+\t\treturn config.KindReviewer, c.String(), true\n+\t}\n+\treturn \"\", \"\", false\n+}\n+\n+// Denied reports whether a cap forbids starting c, and why.\n+func (g *Gate) Denied(c pool.Candidate) (bool, string) {\n+\tkind, stage, ok := kindOf(c.Class)\n+\tif !ok {\n+\t\treturn false, \"\"\n+\t}\n+\tcfg := g.cfg()\n+\tif cfg == nil {\n+\t\treturn false, \"\"\n+\t}\n+\trole, ok := cfg.Role(kind, c.Role)\n+\tif !ok || (kind == config.KindScanner && role.ScanPath != \"\") {\n+\t\treturn false, \"\"\n+\t}\n+\tnow := g.now().UTC()\n+\tdayStart := time.Date(now.Year(), now.Month(), now.Day(), 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)\n+\tctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 10*time.Second)\n+\tdefer cancel()\n+\tper, repo, err := g.store.SpendSince(ctx, dayStart)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tg.log.Error(\"budget.read_failed\", \"error\", err)\n+\t\treturn true, \"budget: spend unreadable, failing closed\"\n+\t}\n+\tif cfg.BudgetUSDPerDay > 0 && repo.USD >= cfg.BudgetUSDPerDay {\n+\t\treturn true, g.capped(dayStart, \"repo\", \"\", repo, cfg.BudgetUSDPerDay)\n+\t}\n+\tif limit, set := cfg.RoleBudgetUSDPerDay(role); set {\n+\t\tsp := per[state.RoleKey{Stage: stage, Role: c.Role}]\n+\t\tif sp.USD >= limit {\n+\t\t\treturn true, g.capped(dayStart, \"role\", stage+\"/\"+c.Role, sp, limit)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\treturn false, \"\"\n+}\n+\n+// capped builds the reason and logs budget.capped once per cap and UTC day.\n+func (g *Gate) capped(dayStart time.Time, scope, role string, sp state.Spend, limit float64) string {\n+\treason := fmt.Sprintf(\"%s daily budget reached: $%.2f of $%.2f spent today (UTC)\", scope, sp.USD, limit)\n+\tif sp.Unpriced > 0 {\n+\t\treason += fmt.Sprintf(\", %d unpriced steps counted as $0\", sp.Unpriced)\n+\t}\n+\tg.mu.Lock()\n+\tif !g.day.Equal(dayStart) {\n+\t\tg.day, g.logged = dayStart, map[string]bool{}\n+\t}\n+\tkey := scope + \"|\" + role\n+\tfirst := !g.logged[key]\n+\tg.logged[key] = true\n+\tg.mu.Unlock()\n+\tif first {\n+\t\tg.log.Warn(\"budget.capped\", \"scope\", scope, \"role\", role, \"spent_usd\", sp.USD, \"cap_usd\", limit, \"unpriced\", sp.Unpriced)\n+\t}\n+\treturn reason\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/budget/budget_test.go b/internal/budget/budget_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..dab1c57\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/budget/budget_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,107 @@\n+package budget\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"bytes\"\n+\t\"context\"\n+\t\"errors\"\n+\t\"log/slog\"\n+\t\"strings\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/pool\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state\"\n+)\n+\n+type fakeReader struct {\n+\tper   map[state.RoleKey]state.Spend\n+\trepo  state.Spend\n+\terr   error\n+\tsince time.Time\n+}\n+\n+func (f *fakeReader) SpendSince(_ context.Context, d time.Time) (map[state.RoleKey]state.Spend, state.Spend, error) {\n+\tf.since = d\n+\treturn f.per, f.repo, f.err\n+}\n+\n+func testCfg() *config.Resolved {\n+\treturn &config.Resolved{BudgetUSDPerDay: 50, Roles: []config.Role{\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindWorker, Name: \"eng\", BudgetUSDPerDay: 10},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindWorker, Name: \"free\"},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindReviewer, Name: \"rev\", BudgetUSDPerDay: 5},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindScanner, Name: \"det\", ScanPath: \".ard/scanners/det/scan\"},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindScanner, Name: \"llm\", PromptPath: \"p\"},\n+\t}}\n+}\n+\n+func cand(c pool.Class, role string) pool.Candidate {\n+\treturn pool.Candidate{Class: c, Role: role, Number: 1, Since: time.Unix(1, 0)}\n+}\n+\n+func setup(r *fakeReader) (*Gate, *bytes.Buffer, *time.Time) {\n+\tnow := time.Date(2026, 3, 4, 23, 59, 0, 0, time.UTC)\n+\tvar buf bytes.Buffer\n+\tg := New(r, testCfg, func() time.Time { return now }, slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(&buf, nil)))\n+\treturn g, &buf, &now\n+}\n+\n+func TestRoleCap(t *testing.T) {\n+\tr := &fakeReader{per: map[state.RoleKey]state.Spend{{Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"eng\"}: {USD: 9.99}}, repo: state.Spend{USD: 9.99}}\n+\tg, _, _ := setup(r)\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"eng\")); d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"just under cap must pass\")\n+\t}\n+\tr.per[state.RoleKey{Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"eng\"}] = state.Spend{USD: 10, Unpriced: 2}\n+\td, why := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"eng\"))\n+\tif !d || !strings.Contains(why, \"2 unpriced\") {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"spent == cap: %v %q\", d, why)\n+\t}\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"free\")); d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"role cap must not deny another role\")\n+\t}\n+\tif !r.since.Equal(time.Date(2026, 3, 4, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)) {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"day start = %v\", r.since)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestRepoCap(t *testing.T) {\n+\tg, _, _ := setup(&fakeReader{repo: state.Spend{USD: 50}})\n+\tfor _, c := range []pool.Candidate{cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"free\"), cand(pool.ClassReview, \"rev\"), cand(pool.ClassScanner, \"llm\")} {\n+\t\tif d, _ := g.Denied(c); !d {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%v must be denied at the repo cap\", c)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassScanner, \"det\")); d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"deterministic scanner must be exempt\")\n+\t}\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"gone\")); d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"role missing from config is exempt\")\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestReaderErrorFailsClosed(t *testing.T) {\n+\tg, _, _ := setup(&fakeReader{err: errors.New(\"db\")})\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"free\")); !d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"read error must deny\")\n+\t}\n+\tif d, _ := g.Denied(cand(pool.ClassScanner, \"det\")); d {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"deterministic scanner needs no read\")\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestCappedLoggedOncePerDay(t *testing.T) {\n+\tg, buf, now := setup(&fakeReader{repo: state.Spend{USD: 60}})\n+\tfor range 3 {\n+\t\tg.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"free\"))\n+\t}\n+\tif n := strings.Count(buf.String(), \"budget.capped\"); n != 1 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"logged %d times, want 1\", n)\n+\t}\n+\t*now = now.Add(2 * time.Minute) // past 00:00 UTC\n+\tg.Denied(cand(pool.ClassWorker, \"free\"))\n+\tif n := strings.Count(buf.String(), \"budget.capped\"); n != 2 {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"logged %d times after rollover, want 2\", n)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/config/budget_test.go b/internal/config/budget_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..e6e33d6\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/config/budget_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,16 @@\n+package config\n+\n+import \"testing\"\n+\n+func TestRoleBudgetUSDPerDay(t *testing.T) {\n+\tr := &Resolved{BudgetUSDPerDay: 50}\n+\tif c, ok := r.RoleBudgetUSDPerDay(Role{BudgetUSDPerDay: 7}); !ok || c != 7 {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"override: %v %v\", c, ok)\n+\t}\n+\tif c, ok := r.RoleBudgetUSDPerDay(Role{}); ok || c != 0 {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"unset: %v %v\", c, ok)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, ok := r.RoleBudgetUSDPerDay(Role{BudgetUSDPerDay: -1}); ok {\n+\t\tt.Error(\"negative override must count as unset\")\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/config/types.go b/internal/config/types.go\nindex ef42cce..94ee2d3 100644\n--- a/internal/config/types.go\n+++ b/internal/config/types.go\n@@ -183,6 +183,16 @@ func (r *Resolved) Role(k Kind, name string) (Role, bool) {\n \treturn Role{}, false\n }\n \n+// RoleBudgetUSDPerDay is the role's own daily cap in USD. set is false when\n+// the role has no override (an override of 0 or less counts as unset); then\n+// only the repo-wide Resolved.BudgetUSDPerDay applies to it.\n+func (r *Resolved) RoleBudgetUSDPerDay(role Role) (cap float64, set bool) {\n+\tif role.BudgetUSDPerDay > 0 {\n+\t\treturn role.BudgetUSDPerDay, true\n+\t}\n+\treturn 0, false\n+}\n+\n // HostUntrusted returns the roles that read untrusted input and have no\n // Dockerfile, so the local executor runs them as a host subprocess under the\n // controller's uid. Canonical order (kind, then name). It returns nothing\ndiff --git a/internal/pool/pool.go b/internal/pool/pool.go\nindex 3c2ee39..21adeea 100644\n--- a/internal/pool/pool.go\n+++ b/internal/pool/pool.go\n@@ -23,9 +23,10 @@ const (\n \tDeniedDuplicate         // same key already held, or earlier in this batch\n \tDeniedInvalid           // malformed candidate (caller bug)\n \tDeniedLimited           // its account or model family is out of quota until a reset time\n+\tDeniedBudget            // a daily dollar cap (its role's or the repo's) is reached\n )\n \n-var verdictNames = [...]string{\"granted\", \"full\", \"outranked\", \"issue-busy\", \"duplicate\", \"invalid\", \"limited\"}\n+var verdictNames = [...]string{\"granted\", \"full\", \"outranked\", \"issue-busy\", \"duplicate\", \"invalid\", \"limited\", \"budget\"}\n \n // String returns the verdict's log name.\n func (v Verdict) String() string {\n@@ -61,6 +62,10 @@ type Options struct {\n \t// now. nil disables the check. A limited candidate takes no slot and\n \t// reserves no demand: it does not hold lower classes back.\n \tLimited func(c Candidate) time.Time\n+\t// Budget reports whether a daily dollar cap forbids starting c, with the\n+\t// reason. nil disables the check. A refused candidate takes no slot and\n+\t// reserves no demand; a job already held is never affected.\n+\tBudget func(c Candidate) (denied bool, reason string)\n }\n \n // Pool is the in-memory slot arbiter. One mutex guards all state; there are\n@@ -72,6 +77,7 @@ type Pool struct {\n \tlog         *slog.Logger\n \tstarveAfter time.Duration\n \tlimited     func(c Candidate) time.Time\n+\tbudget      func(c Candidate) (bool, string)\n \theld        map[string]*Slot\n \tdemand      map[string]*loopDemand\n \tfirstDenied map[string]time.Time\n@@ -86,6 +92,7 @@ func New(o Options) *Pool {\n \t\tlog:         o.Log,\n \t\tstarveAfter: o.StarveAfter,\n \t\tlimited:     o.Limited,\n+\t\tbudget:      o.Budget,\n \t\theld:        map[string]*Slot{},\n \t\tdemand:      map[string]*loopDemand{},\n \t\tfirstDenied: map[string]time.Time{},\n@@ -177,6 +184,12 @@ func (p *Pool) Offer(loop string, ttl time.Duration, cands []Candidate) []Decisi\n \t\t\t\tcontinue\n \t\t\t}\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif p.budget != nil {\n+\t\t\tif denied, why := p.budget(c); denied {\n+\t\t\t\tdecisions[i].Verdict, decisions[i].Reason = DeniedBudget, why\n+\t\t\t\tcontinue\n+\t\t\t}\n+\t\t}\n \t\tinBatch[k] = true\n \t\titems = append(items, item{c, i})\n \t}\ndiff --git a/internal/pool/pool_test.go b/internal/pool/pool_test.go\nindex 99c92cf..6afe9ee 100644\n--- a/internal/pool/pool_test.go\n+++ b/internal/pool/pool_test.go\n@@ -577,3 +577,27 @@ func TestLimitedNilBehavesAsBefore(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tt.Errorf(\"verdict = %v\", ds[0].Verdict)\n \t}\n }\n+\n+func TestBudgetVerdict(t *testing.T) {\n+\tif DeniedBudget.String() != \"budget\" {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"String = %q\", DeniedBudget.String())\n+\t}\n+\tck := &clock{t: t0.Add(1000 * time.Hour)}\n+\theld := Held{Candidate: cand(ClassWorker, \"eng\", 5, 0), JobID: \"j1\"}\n+\tp := New(Options{Max: 2, Now: ck.now, Held: []Held{held}, Budget: func(c Candidate) (bool, string) {\n+\t\treturn c.Role == \"capped\", \"cap hit\"\n+\t}})\n+\tds := p.Offer(\"review\", time.Minute, []Candidate{cand(ClassReview, \"capped\", 9, 0)})\n+\tif ds[0].Verdict != DeniedBudget || ds[0].Slot != nil || ds[0].Reason != \"cap hit\" {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"decision = %+v\", ds[0])\n+\t}\n+\t// A refused reviewer reserves nothing: a worker takes the free slot.\n+\tif ds := p.Offer(\"w\", time.Minute, []Candidate{cand(ClassWorker, \"eng\", 6, 0)}); ds[0].Verdict != Granted {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"worker verdict = %v\", ds[0].Verdict)\n+\t}\n+\t// A held key stays a duplicate even though its role is capped.\n+\tp2 := New(Options{Max: 2, Now: ck.now, Held: []Held{held}, Budget: func(Candidate) (bool, string) { return true, \"x\" }})\n+\tif ds := p2.Offer(\"w\", time.Minute, []Candidate{cand(ClassWorker, \"eng\", 5, 0)}); ds[0].Verdict != DeniedDuplicate {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"held verdict = %v\", ds[0].Verdict)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/adopt.go b/internal/servecmd/adopt.go\nindex 1098908..e072d63 100644\n--- a/internal/servecmd/adopt.go\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/adopt.go\n@@ -55,14 +55,15 @@ func heldJobs(ctx context.Context, l jobLister) ([]pool.Held, error) {\n \n // adoptPool builds the slot pool holding every job already running. limited,\n // when non-nil, is the pool's Options.Limited: it keeps candidates whose\n-// account or model family is out of quota from starting.\n-func adoptPool(ctx context.Context, l jobLister, maxPods int, log *slog.Logger, limited func(pool.Candidate) time.Time) (*pool.Pool, error) {\n+// account or model family is out of quota from starting. budget, when\n+// non-nil, is the pool's Options.Budget: it refuses candidates at a daily cap.\n+func adoptPool(ctx context.Context, l jobLister, maxPods int, log *slog.Logger, limited func(pool.Candidate) time.Time, budget func(pool.Candidate) (bool, string)) (*pool.Pool, error) {\n \theld, err := heldJobs(ctx, l)\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\treturn nil, err\n \t}\n \tlog.Info(\"pool.adopt\", \"held\", len(held), \"max\", maxPods)\n-\treturn pool.New(pool.Options{Max: maxPods, Held: held, Log: log, Limited: limited}), nil\n+\treturn pool.New(pool.Options{Max: maxPods, Held: held, Log: log, Limited: limited, Budget: budget}), nil\n }\n \n // scannerJobStore is what retiring stale scanner jobs needs; *state.Store\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/adopt_test.go b/internal/servecmd/adopt_test.go\nindex 1919baf..0b6e431 100644\n--- a/internal/servecmd/adopt_test.go\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/adopt_test.go\n@@ -67,14 +67,14 @@ func TestAdoptPoolHoldsRunningJobs(t *testing.T) {\n \t\t{ID: \"a\", Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"w\", Issue: 1, CreatedAt: at},\n \t\t{ID: \"b\", Stage: \"worker\", Role: \"w\", Issue: 2, CreatedAt: at},\n \t}}\n-\tp, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), l, 2, slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil)), nil)\n+\tp, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), l, 2, slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil)), nil, nil)\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t}\n \tif st := p.Stats(); st.Held != 2 || st.Free != 0 {\n \t\tt.Errorf(\"stats = %+v\", st)\n \t}\n-\tif _, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), lister{err: errors.New(\"db\")}, 2, nil, nil); err == nil {\n+\tif _, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), lister{err: errors.New(\"db\")}, 2, nil, nil, nil); err == nil {\n \t\tt.Error(\"want lister error\")\n \t}\n }\n@@ -120,7 +120,7 @@ func TestStaleScannerJobsAreFailedNotHeld(t *testing.T) {\n \t\tt.Errorf(\"held = %+v, %v: a scanner job must never be held\", held, err)\n \t}\n \t// With the stale job out of the way the scanner can be offered a slot.\n-\tp, _ := adoptPool(context.Background(), r, 2, log, nil)\n+\tp, _ := adoptPool(context.Background(), r, 2, log, nil, nil)\n \td := p.Offer(\"scanners\", time.Minute, []pool.Candidate{{Class: pool.ClassScanner, Role: \"cve\", Since: at}})\n \tif d[0].Verdict != pool.Granted {\n \t\tt.Errorf(\"verdict = %v\", d[0].Verdict)\n@@ -129,7 +129,7 @@ func TestStaleScannerJobsAreFailedNotHeld(t *testing.T) {\n \n func TestAdoptPoolPassesLimited(t *testing.T) {\n \tuntil := time.Now().Add(time.Hour)\n-\tp, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), lister{}, 2, slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil)), func(pool.Candidate) time.Time { return until })\n+\tp, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), lister{}, 2, slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil)), func(pool.Candidate) time.Time { return until }, nil)\n \tif err != nil {\n \t\tt.Fatal(err)\n \t}\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/budget.go b/internal/servecmd/budget.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..476de6d\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/budget.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,23 @@\n+package servecmd\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/budget\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n+)\n+\n+// budgetGate returns the one daily-budget gate of this process, built on first\n+// use from the store and the latest loaded config.\n+func (w *wiring) budgetGate() *budget.Gate {\n+\tw.initMu.Lock()\n+\tdefer w.initMu.Unlock()\n+\tif w.bgt == nil {\n+\t\tnow := w.budgetNow\n+\t\tif now == nil {\n+\t\t\tnow = time.Now\n+\t\t}\n+\t\tw.bgt = budget.New(w.Store, func() *config.Resolved { return w.cfg.Load() }, now, w.Log)\n+\t}\n+\treturn w.bgt\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/budget_test.go b/internal/servecmd/budget_test.go\nnew file mode 100644\nindex 0000000..63fc8b0\n--- /dev/null\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/budget_test.go\n@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@\n+package servecmd\n+\n+import (\n+\t\"context\"\n+\t\"io\"\n+\t\"log/slog\"\n+\t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n+\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/pool\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/state\"\n+)\n+\n+type budgetClock struct{ t time.Time }\n+\n+func (c *budgetClock) now() time.Time { return c.t }\n+\n+func budgetConfig() *config.Resolved {\n+\treturn &config.Resolved{BudgetUSDPerDay: 20, Roles: []config.Role{\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindWorker, Name: \"eng\", BudgetUSDPerDay: 5},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindWorker, Name: \"other\"},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindReviewer, Name: \"rev\"},\n+\t\t{Kind: config.KindScanner, Name: \"det\", ScanPath: \"x/scan\"},\n+\t}}\n+}\n+\n+func budgetWiring(t *testing.T, dir string, clk *budgetClock) (*wiring, *state.Store) {\n+\tt.Helper()\n+\tctx := context.Background()\n+\tst, err := state.Open(ctx, dir, state.Options{Now: clk.now})\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tt.Cleanup(func() { st.Close() })\n+\tw := &wiring{Log: slog.New(slog.NewTextHandler(io.Discard, nil)), Store: st, budgetNow: clk.now}\n+\tw.cfg.Store(budgetConfig())\n+\treturn w, st\n+}\n+\n+func budgetPool(t *testing.T, w *wiring, st *state.Store) *pool.Pool {\n+\tt.Helper()\n+\tp, err := adoptPool(context.Background(), st, 4, w.Log, nil, w.budgetGate().Denied)\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\treturn p\n+}\n+\n+func spend(t *testing.T, st *state.Store, stage, role string, issue int, step string, usd float64) {\n+\tt.Helper()\n+\tj, err := st.CreateJob(context.Background(), state.NewJob{Stage: stage, Role: role, Issue: issue})\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tif _, err := st.RecordStepUsage(context.Background(), j.ID, step, state.StepUsage{CostUSD: &usd}); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func bc(c pool.Class, role string, n int) pool.Candidate {\n+\treturn pool.Candidate{Class: c, Role: role, Number: n, Since: time.Unix(1, 0)}\n+}\n+\n+func verdict(p *pool.Pool, c pool.Candidate) pool.Verdict {\n+\treturn p.Offer(\"t\", time.Minute, []pool.Candidate{c})[0].Verdict\n+}\n+\n+func TestBudgetGateServeLevel(t *testing.T) {\n+\tclk := &budgetClock{t: time.Date(2026, 3, 4, 12, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)}\n+\tdir := t.TempDir()\n+\tw, st := budgetWiring(t, dir, clk)\n+\tp := budgetPool(t, w, st)\n+\n+\t// A job holds a slot before the caps are reached.\n+\tslot := p.Offer(\"w\", time.Minute, []pool.Candidate{bc(pool.ClassWorker, \"eng\", 1)})[0].Slot\n+\tif slot == nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(\"first offer not granted\")\n+\t}\n+\n+\t// Role cap: only that role is refused.\n+\tspend(t, st, \"worker\", \"eng\", 1, \"a\", 5)\n+\tif v := verdict(p, bc(pool.ClassWorker, \"eng\", 2)); v != pool.DeniedBudget {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"eng at cap = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+\tif v := verdict(p, bc(pool.ClassWorker, \"other\", 3)); v != pool.Granted {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"other = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+\t// The running job still finishes: releasing its slot works.\n+\tslot.Release()\n+\n+\t// Repo cap: every agent role is refused, the deterministic scanner is not.\n+\tspend(t, st, \"worker\", \"other\", 3, \"a\", 15)\n+\tfor _, c := range []pool.Candidate{bc(pool.ClassWorker, \"other\", 4), bc(pool.ClassReview, \"rev\", 5)} {\n+\t\tif v := verdict(p, c); v != pool.DeniedBudget {\n+\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%v = %v, want budget\", c, v)\n+\t\t}\n+\t}\n+\tif v := verdict(p, bc(pool.ClassScanner, \"det\", 0)); v != pool.Granted {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"deterministic scanner = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+\n+\t// Restart: new wiring and pool on the same store still deny.\n+\tw2, st2 := budgetWiring(t, dir, clk)\n+\tst.Close()\n+\tp2 := budgetPool(t, w2, st2)\n+\tif v := verdict(p2, bc(pool.ClassReview, \"rev\", 6)); v != pool.DeniedBudget {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"after restart = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+\n+\t// 00:00 UTC clears the caps.\n+\tclk.t = time.Date(2026, 3, 5, 0, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)\n+\tif v := verdict(p2, bc(pool.ClassReview, \"rev\", 7)); v != pool.Granted {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"after midnight = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestBudgetGateFailsClosedOnReadError(t *testing.T) {\n+\tclk := &budgetClock{t: time.Date(2026, 3, 4, 12, 0, 0, 0, time.UTC)}\n+\tw, st := budgetWiring(t, t.TempDir(), clk)\n+\tp := budgetPool(t, w, st)\n+\tst.Close()\n+\tif v := verdict(p, bc(pool.ClassWorker, \"other\", 1)); v != pool.DeniedBudget {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"closed store = %v, want budget\", v)\n+\t}\n+\tif v := verdict(p, bc(pool.ClassScanner, \"det\", 0)); v != pool.Granted {\n+\t\tt.Errorf(\"deterministic scanner = %v\", v)\n+\t}\n+}\n+\n+func TestBudgetLoopNameAccepted(t *testing.T) {\n+\tif _, err := selectLoops(nil, []string{\"budget\"}); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+}\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/loops_test.go b/internal/servecmd/loops_test.go\nindex 74057f2..42e882d 100644\n--- a/internal/servecmd/loops_test.go\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/loops_test.go\n@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ func TestServeUnknownLoopFailsFirst(t *testing.T) {\n \n // The loop names registerLoops' constructors report are the known names.\n func TestKnownLoopsMatchLoopNames(t *testing.T) {\n-\twant := []string{\"scanners\", \"stale\", \"triage\", \"workers\", \"reviewers\", \"reviewfix\", \"merger\", \"reaper\"}\n+\twant := []string{\"scanners\", \"stale\", \"triage\", \"workers\", \"reviewers\", \"reviewfix\", \"merger\", \"reaper\", \"budget\"}\n \tif !reflect.DeepEqual(knownLoops, want) {\n \t\tt.Errorf(\"knownLoops = %v, want %v\", knownLoops, want)\n \t}\ndiff --git a/internal/servecmd/serve.go b/internal/servecmd/serve.go\nindex 2a56753..ce696c7 100644\n--- a/internal/servecmd/serve.go\n+++ b/internal/servecmd/serve.go\n@@ -20,6 +20,7 @@ import (\n \t\"time\"\n ","numLines":706,"startLine":1,"totalLines":894,"truncatedByTokenCap":true}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019ZgXVoKucyxmqCWVcRSrgk","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
